body.text:
Exploit the mature lowering theorem using BonfyreStitch and the reconciled Operator ABI. Stitch already walks an operator DAG backward, materializes missing realizations, caches by node_hash, and can compile recipes to a standalone C driver. Use that capability for communication density.

For one repeated deterministic/ref-heavy handoff pattern from this live mesh (for example: semantic ref -> query lens -> normalized identity set -> evidence digest/parity check), represent it as a typed SemanticProgram whose operators are exact/pure or explicitly governed. When execution evidence shows the program is stable:
1. lower it through the existing realization/materialization owner;
2. compile a content-addressed native microprogram (Stitch/current owner if lawful);
3. bind it to the exact Operator ABI versions + basis/validity vector;
4. another worker receives only program_ref + input semantic refs + expected output contract;
5. execute locally and emit Process/UsageOccurrence + evidence;
6. compare native result with the high-level semantic derivation;
7. use the compiled form while assumptions remain valid; automatically fall back/recompile when operator contracts, authority/effect class, information policy or proof requirements change.

Never compile away Authority/Evidence/FinancialFact/hard ConstraintIR checks. Lossy/model-dependent operators must retain their uncertainty/evidence semantics. Compiled program != canonical source. Cache hit != cash saving.

The target is literal communication compression: a mature repeated 'conversation' becomes a tiny executable semantic artifact instead of another LLM prompt.
